From caf-talk Caf June 7 00:00:00 1992
From: sheehan@indiana.edu
Subject: Article 1--Sexual harassment at Vanderbilt
Message-ID: <mcs1.19920624@indiana.edu>
Date: Wed, 24 Jun 1992 16:50:00 EST

This is a brief synopsis of a note pulled from ETHICS-L and posted
to  alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk by Mark P. Neely, Northern Territory 
University. The original message was posted by Elliott Mitchell, and 
reached ETHICS-L with an assist from Rich England.

The Tennessean, a Nashville, TN newspaper, reported on May 30, 1992 on
an incident involving a Vanderbilt Univerity system for electronic
"brainstorming." The incident led to a complaint filed with the U.S.
Department of Education by a student who was anonymously harassed over
the system by other students.

The system, named CM3, allows students to post ideas anonymously to 
a three-way computer screen. Nineteen members of a  Masters of Business
Administration class at the Owen School of Management were participating
in an exercise using the system when, "Unfortunately, some of the
students in the session chose to brainstorm on the CM3 system by keying
in offensive remarks directed toward other members of their class, men
and women," said Martin S. Geisel, dean of the school.

A woman student was the subject of particularly abusive language, some 
of which is reported to have been sexual in character. It is she who
who filed a complaint with Vanderbilt and the U.S. Department of
Education.

The Department of Education is investigating the complaint, and is
hopeful that Vanderbilt will want to bring its procedures into
compliance with Department regulations. Meanwhile, Vanderbilt is
cooperating with the investigation and addressing internally the issues
it raises.

Geisel has termed the incident "regrettable."

-Mark Sheehan
-Technical Communications Administrator
-Indiana University, Bloomington


